WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.540
Secure temporary-file handling starts with the mindset that temporary files are real storage.

2
00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:14.680
They can contain sensitive data, survive longer than intended, and interact with filesystem permissions, names, links, and operational tools.

3
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:16.960
Use trusted locations.

4
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:22.340
Prefer per-user or application-private directories with appropriate permissions.

5
00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:30.680
Treat environment-provided paths as configuration, and avoid casual dependence on shared writable directories unless the design accounts for that safely.

6
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.920
Avoid predictable names and check-then-create logic.

7
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:42.100
Use atomic exclusive creation through platform-supported APIs or well-reviewed wrappers, and keep the opened handle as the authority whenever possible.

8
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.420
Restrict permissions and minimize contents.

9
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:52.560
Do not write secrets, unnecessary sensitive data, or broad diagnostics to disk simply because the file is temporary.

10
00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:58.100
Review inherited permissions and ownership expectations across deployment contexts.

11
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.820
Finally, use RAII cleanup and plan for failure.

12
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:09.840
Temporary-file code should handle partial writes, disk pressure, restarts, orphaned files, and cleanup errors predictably.

13
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.060
Review this code as part of secure C++ engineering, not as incidental plumbing.

14
00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.900
The durable habit is to make temporary-file behavior explicit in code.

15
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:32.080
Location, creation, permissions, contents, lifetime, cleanup, and failure response should be visible enough that reviewers can reason about them without guesswork.
